label:
There are certainly plenty of museums in NYC other than the Met.

Do you have a preference on the type, such as those focused on history, science, culture, or a specific geographical area? 

Here are just a few suggestions:
The 9/11 Memorial & Museum, and located across the street from it is One World Trade Center. It is one of the tallest buildings in the world with observation decks from which you will have excellent views of the city; 
American Museum of Natural History on the west side of Central Park is one of the largest museums in the world, housing exhibits on the planetary, geological, and biological sciences. The museum also features a planetarium; 
The Intrepid Sea, Air, and Space Museum, a structure which previously served as an aircraft carrier in World War II and the Vietnam War; 
Museum of the City of New York; 
Ellis Island Immigration Museum; 
Bronx Museum of the Arts (timed entry reservations required); 
Brooklyn Museum is one of the oldest and largest art museums in the U.S.; 
Guggenheim Museum, which was famously designed by Frank Lloyd Wright;
Museum of Modern Art (MoMA) which also includes exhibits on photography and electronic media; 
The Skyscraper Museum; 
The New York Historical Society is the oldest museum in NYC; and
The Tenement Museum depicts immigrant life in NYC in the late 1800s to early 1900s.
